    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change: shared `householdId` for household members\n\nWhen a create request sets `sharesHousehold=true`, an owner is allowed at an address already used by a same-lastName owner (existing behaviour), and now every owner of that household is assigned the same stable `householdId`, which is returned on `GET`.\n\n**Design choice:** the household id is *derived* \u2014 the SHA-256 hex of the canonical household key (normalized `lastName` + `address`). Because it's a pure function of last name and address, every owner of the same household gets an identical, stable value regardless of creation order, with no cross-owner lookup needed. This keeps the logic cohesive with the existing `HouseholdKey` (which already defines household identity for uniqueness checks) rather than introducing stateful id allocation.\n\n**Files touched:**\n\n- `HouseholdKey.java` \u2014 added `id(lastName, address)`, deriving the stable identifier from the same canonical key already used for `of(...)`. Single responsibility retained: this class owns \"household identity.\"\n- `AssignHouseholdId.java` (new) \u2014 a small POST-pipeline function mirroring `AssignCustomerCode`: takes the built `@Val Owner`, sets `householdId` in place before save.\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 wired `assignHousehold` between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, under the same transaction governance.\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 added the `householdId` field (`@Column(name = \"household_id\")`) with getter/setter.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added `householdId` as a `readOnly` property on the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and MapStruct maps it by name (no mapper change needed, same as `customerCode`).\n- `db/{hsqldb,h2,postgres,mysql}/schema.sql` \u2014 added the `household_id` column.\n\n`EnsureUniqueHousehold` was left unchanged \u2014 it already permits the shared-household case; it just needed a companion step to assign the id.\n\n**Verification:** `AcceptanceTest.coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` passes (two opt-in joiners at the same last name/address return an equal, non-blank `householdId`), and the full suite is green \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
